The native key behind each key event on Windows (Task 4 of docs/plans/20261029-windows-support.md, the Windows form of
gpui-pre-linux's 0001): GPUI's `Keystroke` keeps neither a key's position nor its layout's unshifted character and
drops Shift from symbols, which a terminal needs for physical-key bindings and the Kitty keyboard protocol. The window
procedure installs a `NativeInput` for exactly the dispatch of one key event (an RAII scope): a press (`WM_KEYDOWN`,
`WM_SYSKEYDOWN`, an autorepeat flagged by the previous key state) and its release carry `NativeInput::Key` (the scan
code, `0xE0`-prefixed for an extended key as Ghostty's keycode table holds it, the virtual key, the modifiers with
Shift, those the layout used up, Caps Lock and Num Lock, the layout's unshifted character and whether that layout types
ASCII letters, the text before Ctrl transformations, the dead-key and repeat flags, the keystroke GPUI built).
`current_native_input()` reads it during the dispatch. Additive: no GPUI type changes, so gpui-kit, gpui-component and
the other platforms build unchanged. scripts/vendor-gpui.sh applies this to the verified crates.io archive.

